---
label: Nested lists
---

Rows that are themselves lists: chapters that reorder, each holding articles
that reorder within their chapter and move to another. Project Attributes
(`Settings::ProjectCustomFieldSections`) is the reference. This is the
advanced case; the six rules below are all it adds to the previous tabs.

## Anatomy of a nested root

<img src="<%= image_path("lookbook/sortable_lists_d.png") %>" alt="One root holding a chapter list; each chapter row hosts its own article list; one move endpoint per item type" width="636" style="max-width: 100%">

Chapters reorder among themselves; articles reorder within a chapter and
move between chapters. Still one root: the second level comes from a second
item type, never from a second root. The colors match the diagrams in
**Implementation overview**; the same four controllers, only the types differ.

**One root, two item types, never nested roots.** Depth comes from a second
item type under the same root. The root's outlet selectors match at any
depth, so it already sees both levels; a row belongs to the innermost list
that contains it.

```
[sortable-lists]                      root
 └ [sortable-lists--list]             type: chapter · accepted_type: chapter
    └ [sortable-lists--item]          a chapter, as a row of the chapter list
       └ [sortable-lists--list]       the same chapter, as a list of articles
            type: article · accepted_type: article · id: <chapter id>
          └ [sortable-lists--item]    an article
```

**The item and the list it hosts are different elements.** The chapter row
wraps the article list, usually with ordinary layout markup between them.

```html
<div id="chapters"
     data-controller="sortable-lists"
     data-sortable-lists-move-url-templates-value="<%%= move_url_templates.to_json %>"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--list-outlet="#chapters [data-controller~='sortable-lists--list']"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--item-outlet="#chapters [data-controller~='sortable-lists--item']">
  <div data-controller="sortable-lists--list"
       data-sortable-lists--list-type-value="chapter"
       data-sortable-lists--list-accepted-type-value="chapter">
    <%% chapters.each do |chapter| %>
      <div data-controller="sortable-lists--item"
           data-sortable-lists--item-id-value="<%%= chapter.id %>"
           data-sortable-lists--item-type-value="chapter"
           data-sortable-lists--item-label-value="<%%= chapter.name %>">
        <%%= render(ChapterComponent.new(chapter:)) %>
      </div>
    <%% end %>
  </div>
</div>
```

Inside `ChapterComponent` the same chapter becomes a list:

```html
<div data-controller="sortable-lists--list"
     data-sortable-lists--list-type-value="article"
     data-sortable-lists--list-accepted-type-value="article"
     data-sortable-lists--list-id-value="<%%= chapter.id %>"
     data-sortable-lists--list-name-value="<%%= chapter.name %>"
     data-sortable-lists--item-target="preview">
  <header>
    <button type="button" class="handle" data-sortable-lists--item-target="handle">…</button>
    <%%= chapter.name %>
  </header>
  <ul>…article rows…</ul>
</div>
```

**`accepted_type` keeps the levels apart.** Every list under the root is a
candidate target for every dragged row; the accepted type is the only thing
stopping a chapter from being dropped into a list of articles. A list
without one accepts nothing.

**One move endpoint per item type.** The root supplies `move_url_templates`,
a JSON object keyed by item type, instead of `move_url_template`. The
dragged row's type keys the lookup, because the endpoint belongs to the
record being moved. `move_url_template` remains the fallback for a type
without its own entry.

```ruby
def move_url_templates
  id_placeholder = "__id__"
  {
    chapter: drop_chapter_path(id_placeholder).sub(id_placeholder, "{id}"),
    article: drop_article_path(id_placeholder).sub(id_placeholder, "{id}")
  }
end
```

**The chapter's `handle` and `preview` live inside the article list
element**, because that inner element is what should drag and preview; the
outer row would drag its margins with it. Stimulus binds a target to the
nearest ancestor controller with that identifier and skips the nested
`sortable-lists--item` controllers of the articles, so the chapter's targets
stay the chapter's however deep they sit, and the articles' handles never
leak upward.

**Levels need not share a row tag.** Chapter rows above are `<div>`s directly
under the list element; article lists are `BorderBoxList`s with `<li>` rows
in a `<ul>`. Both resolve without configuration.
